# Dates from engine/notation/chronicle.ts: proto-cuneiform about 3350 to 3200 BC at Uruk, used for
# accounts; oracle bones about 1250 to 1050 BC; Cai Lun's paper in AD 105.
lesson history-the-first-writing v=1 format=teach grade=3 unit=5 subject=history levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "The first writing"
  goal "Say what the first writing was used for, read a museum card of it, and put writing's early steps in order."

  look {
    scene 34x10 {
      row all at=canvas(0, 0) space=1 align=start {
        objectcard card kind=3 name="Clay tablet" use="Counting sheep and grain" year=-3300 about=1 place="Uruk, Iraq" ask=0
        tally one count=12
      }
    }
    say """
      Writing began in the first cities, and at first it was counting. In Uruk, more than five
      thousand years ago, scribes pressed marks into wet clay to record sheep, grain and jars: who
      had given what, and who owed what. Much later, in Shang China, kings had questions scratched
      on bones. Writing changed slowly into signs for words and sounds, and paper came in AD 105.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked history.tablet-tally k=0
    }
    show history.tablet-tally k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show history.writing-card k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.writing-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.tablet-tally k=3 easy-k=2 hard-k=5
    show history.writing-card k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show history.writing-order k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show history.writing-card k=1 easy-k=2 hard-k=0
    show history.writing-gap k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.writing-gap k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    level hard {
      show history.writing-gap k=3 level=medium
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show history.why-write k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show history.invent-signs
  }
  remember {
    say "The first writing was counting: lists of sheep, grain and jars pressed into clay."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that writing was invented, not always there, and that it began for a plain reason:
    cities had more to remember than anyone could hold in their head. The way in adds a scribe's
    tally; the core reads museum cards of a clay tablet and an oracle bone and puts writing's early
    steps in order; the two-star question asks why cities needed writing. The wrong idea to watch
    for is that the first writing was stories; it was accounts. If the child is stuck, keep a
    tally of something at home for a day and ask what they would forget without it. The
    three-star task is for you to mark: the child's own signs, and a message you try to read.
    England's key stage 2 history asks for "the achievements of the earliest civilizations", and
    writing is the first of them.
    Japan's 社会 takes up history only through Japan's own story, in grade six
    (「我が国の歴史上の主な事象について，人物の働きや代表的な文化遺産を中心に」, the course of study as read in its 2008 edition), so
    this lesson has no line of its own there; its timeline and source skills serve that grade.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example adds two small tallies, the card questions ask what the tablet and the bone were for, and the steps of writing are nearly in order."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the tallies are larger, the card questions ask how long before year one each was made, and the steps of writing are fully muddled."
  }
}
